  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 259 PM MDT Sun Aug 9 2026\n\nAs the long term forecast progresses, the 500mb jet moves from the \nPacific NW to right over our area on Wednesday. While this jet \nhas winds in the range of 50-60 knots, it is suppressed. As the \nridge flattens, chances of moisture increase. PWAT values \nincrease starting on Wednesday, with the highest values being \nFriday at around 1.5\" near the Nebraska panhandle. RH values \nincrease significantly on Thursday, ranging from 60-90%! This \nmoisture sticks around through Sunday, even bringing some \nchances of thunderstorms with it. Shear increases Friday and \nSaturday evening, supporting chances of thunderstorms. Surface \nwinds stay in a normal range for the area, typically staying \nbelow 35 knots. With this moisture moving in, and winds staying \nrelatively slow, fire weather becomes much less of a concern \nthrough this long term period. 700mb temperatures show Wednesday\nstill being quite warm, with much of the CWA being in the \n15-17C range. Temperatures begin to cool down Thursday as a cold\nfront moves in, bringing temperatures to the 10-12C range. The \narea stays cool through Sunday, below 13C.",
